  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="commodity_winestone_cbbc3bb87e4fb4ec764b4bd9d584a04b3accd0161ff47ed0936f7dd3cad9463400">
    <crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>winestone, the deposit of crude tartar or argol found in wine-casks (OED s.v. wine n.1 9a) (see also argel, vynstone, winstone); specifically, bitartrate of potash (acid potassium tartrate), present in grape juice, deposited in a crude form in the process of fermentation and adhering to the sides of wine-casks as a hard crust; when purified it becomes cream of tartar (OED s.v. tartar n.1, argol1); used medicinally (MED s.v. argoille, OED s.v. argol1) and in glassmaking | archil, a name given to various species of lichens (Roccella tinctoria, etc.) which yield a violet dye and the chemical test substance litmus; also the colouring-matter prepared from these lichens (OED s.v. archil)</crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>
    <crm:P2_has_type rdf:resource="http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object"/>
  </rdf:Description>
</rdf:RDF>
